<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sebastián Kindelán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sebastián Kindelán y O'Regan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sebastián Kindelán was a Spanish army officer of Irish descent who served as governor of East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and later as captain general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> from 1818 to 1821. His rule fell in the period known as España Boba, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> neglected the impoverished colony. He suppressed conspiracies against Spanish rule, but shortly after his term the colony declared independence and was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kindelán was born on 30 December 1757 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, the son of Vicente Kindelán Luttrell of Luttrellstown and María Francisca O'Regan. His father was an Irishman who had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, joined the infantry of the Royal Spanish Army and risen to brigadier and military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>; his mother came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and was probably also of Irish descent. Kindelán joined the army as a cadet on 18 November 1768 and served in the infantry regiment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 28 March 1799 he became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the whole east of the island. In 1804 influential citizens complained to the Crown that he had encouraged white refugees from the uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> to settle in Cuba, where some twenty thousand or more French immigrants had acquired land and were importing black slaves to work their plantations, and they accused him of irreligion and dishonesty. Kindelán defended the settlers as peaceful.</p><p>Transferred to East Florida in 1811 and promoted brigadier, he was named Royal Governor by the Cádiz Regency on 11 June 1812, the year filibusters from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> tried to seize the province in the Patriot War. He was so angered that he prepared to challenge President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> to a duel until the Captain-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> stopped him. Through black militiamen and interpreters such as Benjamin Wiggins and the enslaved Tony Doctor (Antonio Proctor), he drew the Seminole chiefs King Payne and Bowlegs into the defence; Bowlegs brought two hundred men to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St. Johns River</a>.</p><p>He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> on 3 June 1815. As acting governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> from 1818 he faced Haitians who wished to take over the Spanish part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, and in 1819 he received the Grand Cross of San Fernando for his service in Florida.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Replaced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> by Brigadier Pascual Real in 1821, Kindelán was appointed provisional Captain-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1822 in place of Nicolás Mahy y Romo. His attempt to unite military and civil power in that office set the Spanish troops against the local militia. He served as field marshal from 1824 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Cuba on 4 May 1826. He had married Ana Manuela Mozo de la Torre Garvey in 1801, and the couple had six children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sebastián Kindelán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sánchez Ramírez governed until his death in 1811, followed by captains general such as Carlos de Urrutia, mocked as 'Carlos Conuco' for making soldiers farm, and <b>Sebastián Kindelán</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/espana-boba">España Boba</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in Santiago de Cuba, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.89,-5.3&amp;year=1816" aria-label="Ceuta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4416 868 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sebastián Kindelán"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4866 1138h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4866 1138h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.89,-5.3&amp;year=1816">What was here in 1816? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
